  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "Find the fourth wall",
  "description": "An area of about 50 × 25 metres is split into two halves. Two teams play 4 v 4 in one half. Around it stand three wall players (J) – one on each long side and one at the end – who play with the team in possession. A fourth wall player stands at the far end of the other half.\n\nAfter a number of passes within the team (five, for example), the team must find the fourth wall far away. Play then moves to the other half, and the wall players on the sides go with it. The team that played the ball keeps it and plays on. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Give width: get free out by the touchlines."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Give depth: run in behind, or offer support so the player on the ball can play straight or diagonally back."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Use the walls to switch play when it gets crowded."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "The long pass should be firm and along the ground."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
  "phase": "main",
  "difficulty": 3,
